ncy. That's needed for the evidence quote. Then write notes and report.\n\nBut wait — the required quote for reasoning needs both self_consistency_selection and tot_search. tot_search quote is available on page 13: \"The paper describes both a breadth-first and a depth-first controller.\" Self Consistency rule from page 12: typically \"the answer that occurs most frequently among the sampled paths (majority vote)\". Need exact.\n\nLet me read page 12 once.\n\nActually delivery is bounded; reading page 12 will deliver text of page 12. Fine — one read_pdf call.\n\nAlso note multi_agent note not yet written (CAMEL prompting, Generative Agents evaluators). I need those from pages around 30-40. Hmm, but the recent tool result shows a read_pdf of 46-48. multi_agent note still missing. That means I need pages with CAMEL. That's another read.\n\nI have 7 requests left. Plan: 1 read for page 12 (reasoning quote), 1-2 reads for multi_agent pages (CAMEL & Generative Agents), then 5 notes + report + pytest = ~8 calls. Too many. Hmm, but \"requests_left: 7\" might be model API requests budget within a different counter. Risky.\n\nMaybe more efficient: do read_pdf(12) and read_pdf for multi-agent pages in a single action block (two calls) then write notes. But note writing requires the text in response.\n\nActually notes must be written via write_note which are not model requests (tool calls, but not API model requests?). The max_model_calls=40 counts model requests. Requests_left 7 suggests only 7 API requests remain. Each of my action turns is one model request. So I have 7 turns.\n\nSo: Turn A: read page 12 + multi-agent pages (need to guess pages; CAMEL and Generative Agents likely in section 3 \"interacting\" around pages 30-38). Hmm, guessing is risky. The paper: Section 3 multi-agent? Actually taxonomy: section 2 reasoning, section 3 acting, section 4 interacting. Pages: tool_use p.23, so acting ~ p.18-30, interacting ~ p.31-45.\n\nGenerative Agents evaluators: in the paper, Generative Agents evaluation had 100 human evaluators? Actually the original paper used 100 participants. Hmm.\n\nLet me search instead: could I use run_command with grep on a saved text? I don't think full text is saved to a file I can grep... Actually read_pdf.py extracts each call. But maybe there is a cached text? No.\n\nAlternative: use run_command with pdftotext to extract full text into stdout then grep? That would be a command_result, not required, but the reading requirement demands read_pdf.py for all 52 pages (already done). Using grep to find keywords is a clever way to locate pages cheaply without spending model context. run_command output could be large but I can pipe? No shell. pdftotext writes to file — but \"不要在工作副本中新增缓存文件\" (don't add cache files). Hmm, that would violate.\n\nBut I can run: python -c ... with pdftotext? Not allowed shell. I could run read_pdf.py via run_command? That prints full JSON - large.\n\nBetter: run_command python read_pdf.py --start-page 12 ... and grep? No shell pipes.\n\nHmm, allowed: run_command(argv) — python -c with subprocess to pdftotext and search keyword, printing only page numbers. That's using python -c, which is one command. But os/pathlib unavailable in action code — inside run_command's python script it's fine? The restriction is on action code. Using `python -c` with subprocess and re to extract page numbers for keywords like \"CAMEL\" and \"Generative Agents\". That avoids cache files (read into memory via pdftotext - ). pdftotext - - outputs to stdout.\n\nLet me do that in one command: use pdftotext with page separators (form feed \\f) and search \"CAMEL\" and \"Generative Agents\" and \"Self-Consistency\" / \"most consistent\".\n\nThat's efficient. Then next turn read the needed pages via read_pdf for real evidence ids (needed for write_note evidence_ids and quotes).\n\nBut careful: quotes must come from extracted results on those pages — fine.\n\nLet's do the grep command now.\n\nCommand: [\"python\",\"-c\", \"import subprocess,re;t=subprocess.run(['pdftotext','-layout','paper.pdf','-'],capture_output=True,text=True).stdout;pages=t.split('\\f');...\"] — quoting inside argv list is fine since no shell; but the action code is Python string, need careful quoting. Use triple-quoted string with single quotes inside.\n\nLet me write:\n\ncode = \"\"\"\nimport subprocess\nt = subprocess.run(['pdftotext','-layout','paper.pdf','-'], capture_output=True, text=True).stdout\npages = t.split('\\f')\nfor kw in ['CAMEL','Generative Agents','Self-Consistency','majority','breadth-first']:\n    hits = [i+1 for i,p in enumerate(pages) if kw in p]\n    print(kw, hits)\n\"\"\"\n\nThen run_command(['python','-c',code],'.').\n\nAlso print surrounding context? Keep small.\n\nAlso need to check whether 'python' resolves. Use the venv python path seen: '/mnt/d/.../.venv/bin/python'. Just use 'python'.\n\nLet me also print snippet lines containing 'CAMEL' limited.\n\nI'll print for each hit page the lines containing keyword, truncated to 200 chars, only for CAMEL and Generative Agents.\n\nDo it."}, "logprobs": null, "finish_reason": "stop"}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 7336, "completion_tokens": 1680, "total_tokens": 9016, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 4224}, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 1444}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens": 4224, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens": 3112}, "system_fingerprint": "bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}, "classification": "ok"}
